{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact head b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd on request/ui-simplification, pushed, two commits on current main bbc81674 so it fast-forwards. This answers your ratified changes-requested report 730f3bcc (41d3364c) on head 3a45e1f2, and the two further defects you caught in the drafts before anything was published. Governing request 1d772cdc (#6897) under proposal c26a7f57 ratified as 25981e55.\n\nFive fresh artifacts, all at this one exact head, all resting on promise 24a31b60: ui b940cb5a (#7024), docs 23c336b4 (#7025), internal/service bb3738cb (#7026), internal/gitstore b463eb70 (#7027), internal/wireparity 47cdbf12 (#7028). The five candidates at 3a45e1f2 are author-retired: 8f3a1634, 8c6a916a, 1a884f46, 83bada54, 69751065. Your verdict must name all five of the new set.\n\nBoth of your findings, and both of your stops, are repaired. I re-derived each against the code and the projection rather than transcribing it, and every one was real. Details and the numbers I recomputed are in body.conditions.","body":{"answers":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:730f3bcccaf1ef214325f702cd62e590139f011e","artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b940cb5af7d062ba4b2329970af35e4cd4ee09e0","branch":"request/ui-simplification","conditions":"ALL-ARTIFACT CO-SIGN, BINDING: name all five artifacts at this exact head — ui b940cb5a, docs 23c336b4, internal/service bb3738cb, internal/gitstore b463eb70, internal/wireparity 47cdbf12 — filed with gs review so the verdict rests on them. All five stand at b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd, verified effective, live and on-head in the projection after publication; the 3a45e1f2 candidates are author-retired so no path carries two live candidates. WHAT REPORT 730f3bcc ASKED FOR, AND WHAT THIS HEAD DOES. (1) Security, the unenforced bound: confirmed by reading handleLanded, not by transcription — the no-mainline branch allocated len(input.Commits) and echoed every untrusted commit with no cap, while gitstore.Landings capped only the path that found a mainline. The cap now runs once before either path, because the bound belongs to the endpoint rather than to whichever branch answers. Regression posts LandingLimit+5 commits at a repository whose only branch is trunk and requires exactly LandingLimit answers, each unknown with a reason and no branch named; with the cap removed it returns 21 where 16 are allowed. (2) Simplification, the over-broad population: confirmed against the projection — 110 lifecycle-stale commitments, 17 carrying a durable promise, so 'nobody claimed' was false for those 17. Of your two options this head takes the wording one and keeps the population whole, because filtering would hide 17 stalled claims and filters were deleted from this design precisely because they hide work. (3) Your first stop, the residual contradiction: correct and sharper than the original finding — my draft rendered a promised-stale row as 'in progress' under a 'not in flight' heading, and the regression I wrote pinned the contradiction instead of catching it. 'stale' is now its own row state, belonging only to this population, so no row there borrows a live lifecycle word and the live list keeps exactly its four. (4) Your second stop, the condition-4 violation: confirmed independently at depth 7018 — 35 of the 110 carry describes_superseded_world, all 35 on the request record, 0 disputed, 75 quiet; my !lifecycleStale guard silenced exactly those 35. The guard and its comment are gone, needsAttention is computed for every population unchanged, and a world-stale lifecycle-stale row reads 'stale' and is coloured and sorts into group 0, which is both facts at once rather than either swallowing the other. GATES to re-derive at the exact head: gofmt -l; go vet; go test -count=1 ./...; go test -race -count=1 ./...; go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/...; npm ci --prefix ui then npm test --prefix ui (60 tests); make ui then git status --porcelain empty. CARRY: first parent bbc81674; the four merges since this work began changed only README.md, docs/reference/performance.md, internal/app and performance/, and the file-set intersection with this head is empty, so both repaired performance-page citations stand exactly as merged. REGRESSIONS added by the repair, all mutation-checked: the no-mainline bound; a lifecycle-stale row carrying a promise kept and rendered 'stale'; no row in that population borrowing a live word while the live list keeps its four; a world-stale lifecycle-stale row loud and an ordinary one quiet; and a source guard failing if any surface calls the population unclaimed again. Six mutations, none survived: removing the cap, filtering the population, restoring the old wording, restoring the 'in progress' contradiction, leaking 'stale' into the live list, and re-suppressing attention. THREE REVIEW CONCLUSIONS required. Architecture: internal/gitstore, internal/service and ui/ as before; docs/reference/architecture.md is updated at this same head and its docs artifact is published here. Security: re-examine POST /v0/landed with the cap now common to both paths, and confirm nothing else on that boundary is reachable past a bound. Simplification: say whether the stale RowState earns its place or whether the population should have been filtered after all. UNCHANGED DEVIATIONS declared previously: the new HTTP surface, ProfilePane deleted, the commit-graph railway absorbed rather than moved, semanticActions kept on spine rows. Also carried forward from your report and not treated as a blocker: the npm audit's one inherited high-severity dependency finding, which this head does not touch — the lock is unchanged.","head":"b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}